### Example 3 (Addressing Structural Pairing)
<context>
[
"Jeremy: Hi"
]
</context>
<target>
"Alex: I see a blue wall."
</target>
<think>
The <target> explicitly mentions "a blue wall." This is a certain, background object with a specified color, so it gets a black outline and is colored blue.
Applying the "Structural Pairing" rule regarding architecture: Since a "wall" is explicitly mentioned, its logical structural pair, a "floor," must be included to ground the scene.
As no color is specified for the floor, it remains white. Since the floor is inferred and not explicitly mentioned, it gets a blue outline.
It is impossible to deduce the kind of scenario, so no other assumed objects will be included.
</think>
